EXAMPLES=[{'num':'25','title':'Concurrent Perpendicular Bisectors','question':'For A(0,0), B(8,6), C(12,0), show that the perpendicular bisectors of all three sides are concurrent.','steps':['The midpoint of BC is (10,3), and C−B = (4,−6). Its bisector is 4(x−10)−6(y−3) = 0, or 2x−3y−11 = 0.','The midpoint of CA is (6,0), and A−C = (−12,0). Its bisector is x = 6.','The midpoint of AB is (4,3), and B−A = (8,6). Its bisector is 8(x−4)+6(y−3) = 0, or 4x+3y−25 = 0.','Intersect x = 6 with 2x−3y−11 = 0: 12−3y−11 = 0, giving y = 1/3.','At G(6,1/3), the third bisector gives 24+1−25 = 0, so all three are concurrent at this finite point.','The squared distances from G to A, B and C are respectively 36+1/9, 4+289/9 and 36+1/9; all equal 325/9.','Thus G is the circumcenter, equidistant from all three vertices, independently confirming the bisectors.','The source determinant is also zero: 1728−144−1584 = 0. The directly found finite intersection supplies the condition that determinant zero alone cannot guarantee for arbitrary parallel lines.'],'answer':'Concurrent at the circumcenter G(6,1/3).','progressiveSolution':True,'solutionOrigin':'SpaceBook learning enhancement','sourceReferences':[{'pageStart':204,'pageEnd':205,'verification':'All source vertices, determinant entries and expansion visually checked'}]}]
